WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.189 --> 00:00:04.230
There is nothing like a hot cup of tea in
the morning.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:08.360
That is, until I’m done, and I have to
decide what to do with my cup.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:09.360
Can I recycle it?

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.530
Is that a trick question?

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.040
Yeah this is a great question.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.519
I just figure you can recycle anything that’s
cardboard-esque.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.800
I can’t recycle this?

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.500
Knowing what you can and can’t recycle isn’t
easy.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:31.660
The rules depend on where you live, and there
are hundreds of products and materials where

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:33.500
the rules aren’t always clear.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:36.880
I hear that that's not recyclable.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.500
I don’t know if that's like a legend,
an urban legend or something.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.980
My roommate and I actually have this discussion
where I’m like I’ll throw paper towels

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.760
in there and she’s like ‘I don’t think
you can recycle them’ and pick them out.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.000
Like, I don't know, it's paper.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.140
I don’t know, this is so hard!

00:00:55.140 --> 00:01:00.629
The confusion means things that are actually
garbage still end up in the recycling stream.

00:01:00.629 --> 00:01:05.730
About 25% of what Americans try to recycle,
can’t actually be recycled.

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:10.670
Waste management experts say what's going
on here is something called "aspirational

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:15.930
recycling": When people are unsure if an item
can be recycled, they recycle it, because it

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:22.280
feels like the right thing to do.
And while our intentions are good, this behavior isn't harmless.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.780
Even small amounts of contamination can turn
entire hauls of otherwise recyclable materials

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.880
into trash -- and the problem has been growing.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.900
The rate of recycling contamination more

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:37.230
than doubled in the last decade.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.380
So why is this happening?

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:44.680
Well, it is at least in part due to a major
shift in how Americans recycle.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.980
Beginning in the 1990s and 2000s, municipalities
implemented “single stream” recycling programs.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:56.280
Paper, metal, plastic and glass no longer
needed to be sorted.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.370
They could all live in one bin.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:04.550
Communities quickly adopted the practice and
by 2014, 80% of all curbside recycling programs

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.230
in the US were single stream.

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:12.390
The problem is, there's evidence that when
we put all our recycling into one bin, we're

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.190
more likely to throw trash in there along
with it.

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:19.970
Take two neighboring counties in Florida,
for example: Palm Beach County, where residents

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:26.050
must pre-sort their recyclables, had a contamination
rate of only 9 percent, while Broward County’s

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:29.360
single-stream program had a contamination
rate of 30 percent.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:34.780
Single-stream recycling takes the responsibility
to sort off of the individual, and shifts

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:40.140
it to Materials Recovery Facilities, or MRFs,
where trash gets sorted out from recycling

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.620
by machines, but also by workers, who often
have to remove waste by hand: pizza boxes

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:50.270
contaminated with grease, electronics that
aren’t processed at standard recycling facilities,

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:55.260
even the likes of Christmas lights, animal
carcasses, and bowling balls.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.500
In Portland, workers remove thousands of dirty
diapers every month.

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:07.459
In a perfect world, everyone would know how
to recycle correctly, but short of that, there's

00:03:07.459 --> 00:03:13.590
something we can all start doing differently
right now: Unless you are absolutely sure,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:15.120
don’t recycle it.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.849
In fact, recycling education campaigns encourage
the opposite: when in doubt, the best option

00:03:20.849 --> 00:03:22.660
may be to throw it out.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:27.120
Most people want to do the right thing, and
sometimes the way to be a good recycler is

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.740
to throw stuff in the trash.

